/* Lesson quizzes — batch 10 */
window.LQ=window.LQ||{};
Object.assign(window.LQ,{
"p24m1":[
 {id:"p24m1q1", q:"A fiber has a core index n1 = 1.470 and a cladding index n2 = 1.455. Which rays are trapped by total internal reflection at the core–cladding boundary?", options:["Rays meeting the boundary at less than about 81.8° from the normal","Rays meeting the boundary at more than about 81.8° from the normal","Rays meeting the boundary at more than about 8.2° from the normal","Every ray, as long as the cladding is undamaged"], answer:1, why:"sin θc = n2 ÷ n1 = 1.455 ÷ 1.470 = 0.990, so θc ≈ 81.8°. Only rays striking beyond the critical angle (grazing, within about 8° of the axis) reflect totally; rays closer to the normal refract into the cladding and leak."},
 {id:"p24m1q2", q:"A span measures 16 dB of loss end to end. Roughly what share of the launched power reaches the far end?", options:["About 6.25%","About 16%","About 25%","About 2.5%"], answer:3, why:"Break 16 dB into 10 + 3 + 3: the 10 dB leaves one tenth and each 3 dB halves what is left, so 1/10 × 1/2 × 1/2 = 1/40, about 2.5%. Reading 16 dB as a factor of 16 treats a logarithmic ratio as a linear one."},
 {id:"p24m1q3", q:"An OLT launches +4 dBm. The ONT's meter reads −21 dBm and its receiver sensitivity is −28 dBm. What are the link loss and the remaining margin?", options:["Loss 25 dB; margin 7 dB","Loss 17 dB; margin 11 dB","Loss 25 dB; margin 3 dB","Loss −17 dBm; margin 7 dB"], answer:0, why:"Loss is launch minus received: +4 − (−21) = 25 dB. Margin is received level minus sensitivity: −21 − (−28) = 7 dB. Subtracting magnitudes (21 − 4 = 17) drops the sign, and a loss is a ratio in dB, never a level in dBm."},
 {id:"p24m1q4", q:"Rayleigh scattering varies as 1/λ⁴. About how much more does 1310 nm light scatter than 1550 nm light in the same glass?", options:["About 1.2 times","About 11 times","About 2 times","About 1.4 times"], answer:2, why:"(1550 ÷ 1310)⁴ = 1.183⁴ ≈ 1.96, so roughly twice as much. 1.2 is the plain wavelength ratio without the fourth power, 1.4 is only its square, and 11 is the lecture's 850-versus-1550 nm figure."},
 {id:"p24m1q5", q:"A 10 Gb/s, 1550 nm link over 120 km of G.652 fiber arrives 6 dB above sensitivity and below overload, yet shows heavy bit errors. A colleague proposes a hotter transmitter. Why will that not help?", options:["Higher launch power raises Rayleigh scattering in proportion, so the errors stay","Modal dispersion in single-mode fiber grows with launch power","The errors come from absorption at the 1383 nm water peak","Chromatic dispersion has smeared the pulses in time; brighter pulses still overlap"], answer:3, why:"At about 17 ps/nm/km, 120 km of G.652 spreads 1550 nm pulses until neighboring bits overlap, however bright they are. The cures are compensation or coherent DSP, a lower rate or a shorter span. Single-mode fiber has no modal dispersion at all."}
],
"p24m2":[
 {id:"p24m2q1", q:"A cable uses 12-fiber tubes in the TIA-598 color order, and tubes 13–24 repeat the colors with a tracer. Where is fiber 200?", options:["Tube 16 (brown with tracer), black fiber","Tube 17 (slate with tracer), yellow fiber","Tube 17 (slate with tracer), black fiber","Tube 5 (slate, no tracer), black fiber"], answer:2, why:"Tubes 1–16 hold 16 × 12 = 192 fibers, so fiber 200 is the 8th fiber (black) in tube 17. Tube 17 is the 5th color, slate, in the second set, so it carries a tracer. Rounding 200 ÷ 12 down lands one tube too early."},
 {id:"p24m2q2", q:"On the as-built, the previous access point's Sequential Out was 14,620. At this handhole the cable shows Sequential IN 13,795 and Sequential Out 13,765. What are the running line into this handhole and the slack in it?", options:["Running line 825 ft; slack 30 ft","Running line 855 ft; slack 30 ft","Running line 30 ft; slack 825 ft","Running line 825 ft; slack 0 ft"], answer:0, why:"Running line = previous Out − this IN = 14,620 − 13,795 = 825 ft. Slack = this IN − this Out = 13,795 − 13,765 = 30 ft. Using this hole's Out for the running line (855 ft) counts the coil twice."},
 {id:"p24m2q3", q:"The prints place two handholes at STA 10+60 and STA 19+90. Your sheet shows 905 ft of running line between them. What should you conclude?", options:["It is normal: running line usually sits a little under the stationing distance","It is fine: the 25 ft gap is the slack coiled in the second handhole","Update the stationing on the prints to match the sheet","A footage mark is probably wrong: running line cannot be shorter than 930 ft"], answer:3, why:"Stationing distance is 1,990 − 1,060 = 930 ft. Running line should match or slightly exceed it because of routing and dips, never fall short, so 905 ft points to a misread or mistyped sequential mark. Slack is recorded separately and never explains a short run."},
 {id:"p24m2q4", q:"A 0.75-inch-diameter OSP cable is being placed. What minimum radius do you need on the pulling sheave, and for the coil racked in the vault afterward?", options:["7.5 in at the sheave; 15 in in the vault","15 in at the sheave; 7.5 in in the vault","15 in for both, since the rule never relaxes","30 in at the sheave; 15 in in the vault"], answer:1, why:"The rule is 20 × diameter under tension and 10 × at rest: 20 × 0.75 = 15 in while pulling and 10 × 0.75 = 7.5 in coiled. Reversing them puts the tighter bend on the cable while it is under load, when it is most vulnerable."},
 {id:"p24m2q5", q:"A foreman proposes skipping the 30 ft slack loops in intermediate handholes to save cable on a long run. What is the strongest objection?", options:["The loops keep the fiber under light tension so it cannot drift inside the buffer tube","Each loop lowers attenuation by relieving the stress left in the glass from the pull","Future repairs, relocations or splices there would need new cable instead of spare length","Without the loops, the as-built totals can no longer reconcile against the stationing"], answer:2, why:"Slack is engineered spare length: it lets a crew lift a closure, add a splice or reroute after a dig-in without laying new cable, and it is billed as installed footage. The sheet reconciles with or without slack, because slack is simply counted in the total."}
],
"p24m3":[
 {id:"p24m3q1", q:"Your core-alignment splicer estimates 0.17 dB on one joint. The carrier spec is 0.10 dB average or better, with no splice above 0.15 dB. What do you do?", options:["Accept it: TIA-568 allows up to 0.3 dB per splice","Accept it and let the OTDR decide, since the estimate means nothing","Break it, re-strip, re-clean and recleave, then splice again","Add a second heat-shrink protector to bring the loss down"], answer:2, why:"The estimate is not a measurement, but it is a useful flag: 0.17 dB predicts a failure against the 0.15 dB ceiling, and a poor cleave is the usual cause. Redoing it costs minutes. The TIA 0.3 dB maximum is irrelevant when the contract is stricter."},
 {id:"p24m3q2", q:"After a cabinet swap, one GPON subscriber's receive level drops by about 4 dB. Both end faces inspect clean and the OTDR shows no new bend. At the splitter you find a blue SC jumper in that customer's path. Most likely cause?", options:["An APC-to-UPC mismatch: the 8° face meets a flat face across an air gap","A dirty connector the probe missed, because a 9 µm core hides small particles","A bad fusion splice in the cabinet's pigtail tray","Modal dispersion from the short jumper"], answer:0, why:"Blue housings are UPC, and PON plant is SC/APC (green). Mating an 8° ferrule to a flat one leaves a gap that costs decibels and can damage both faces. Clean end faces and a quiet OTDR trace rule out dirt and splices. Green mates only green."},
 {id:"p24m3q3", q:"A storm cuts a 12-fiber drop cable at 2 a.m. There is no generator or splice trailer, and customers are down. What is the sound plan?", options:["Mechanical splices now, left permanently, since their loss matches fusion","Field-polish epoxy connectors onto every fiber at the break","Wait for daylight and power, since mechanical splices are not allowed outside","Mechanical splices to restore service now, then replace them with fusion splices"], answer:3, why:"Mechanical splices need no power and take minutes, which suits restoration, but they lose 0.2–0.5 dB and their index gel ages and reflects. Scheduling fusion replacement prevents the degraded-gel failure the lessons describe. They are not equivalent to fusion splices."},
 {id:"p24m3q4", q:"A 400× video probe shows a small pit in zone C (adhesive) and one particle sitting on the core (zone A). What next?", options:["Pass it: one small particle is allowed in zone A by count","Dry-clean with a one-click cleaner, then re-inspect before connecting","Reject the connector because zone C has a defect","Wet-clean once with alcohol and connect, since a wet clean always removes particles"], answer:1, why:"Under IEC 61300-3-35, zone A must be flawless while zone C is ignored, so the particle fails and the pit does not matter. Start with a dry one-click clean, re-inspect, and go to wet-dry only if needed. Never connect without re-inspecting after cleaning."},
 {id:"p24m3q5", q:"Why does an 8° APC polish give better return loss (about −60 to −70 dB) than a UPC polish (about −50 to −55 dB)?", options:["The angle widens the contact area, so less light escapes the joint","APC ferrules use lower-index glass that reflects less at the interface","Reflected light leaves at an angle, escapes into the cladding and is not guided back","The angle removes the glass–air interface entirely, so no light can reflect back at all"], answer:2, why:"Some light still reflects at the end face, but on an angled face the reflection falls outside the core's acceptance angle, leaks into the cladding and is lost. On a flat UPC dome it heads straight back down the core. The reflection exists in both; only UPC guides it home."}
],
"p24m4":[
 {id:"p24m4q1", q:"Using TIA-568.3 maximums, what is the acceptable loss for a 6.4 km outside-plant single-mode link with five splices and a mated connector pair at each end?", options:["3.1 dB","4.7 dB","9.4 dB","6.2 dB"], answer:3, why:"Fiber 6.4 × 0.5 = 3.2 dB, splices 5 × 0.3 = 1.5 dB, connector pairs 2 × 0.75 = 1.5 dB: 6.2 dB. 3.1 dB uses typical values (the expected loss, not the limit), 9.4 dB wrongly applies the 1.0 dB/km inside-plant figure, and 4.7 dB forgets the connectors."},
 {id:"p24m4q2", q:"A tech references the OLTS with the launch cord and receive cord mated together, then inserts the link between them. Compared with a one-cord reference, the reported loss will be:", options:["Lower by roughly one connector pair's loss","Higher by roughly one connector pair's loss","The same, because reference methods matter only for multimode","Lower by both end connectors' loss"], answer:0, why:"Loss = reference − measurement. A mated pair inside the reference is subtracted out, so one of the link's two end connections is excluded and the result reads low (the two-cord method). TIA-568 requires the one-cord reference, which includes both; excluding both is the three-cord method."},
 {id:"p24m4q3", q:"An OTDR set to a group index of 1.468 sees a reflection return 25 µs after the pulse leaves. How far away is the event?", options:["About 3.75 km","About 2.55 km","About 5.11 km","About 7.5 km"], answer:1, why:"d = c × t ÷ (2n) = (3 × 10⁸ × 25 × 10⁻⁶) ÷ (2 × 1.468) = 7,500 ÷ 2.936 ≈ 2,554 m. Forgetting the factor of 2 for the round trip gives 5.11 km; forgetting the index gives 3.75 km; forgetting both gives 7.5 km."},
 {id:"p24m4q4", q:"A fusion splice reads −0.08 dB shot from end A and +0.20 dB shot from end B. What loss belongs on the splice sheet?", options:["−0.08 dB, because the splice adds a little gain","0.20 dB, because you always report the worse direction","0.06 dB, the bidirectional average","0.14 dB, half the difference between the readings"], answer:2, why:"The negative reading is a gainer, a backscatter artifact from fibers that scatter differently, not added power. The true loss is the average of both directions: (−0.08 + 0.20) ÷ 2 = 0.06 dB. Half the difference (0.14) reflects the backscatter mismatch, not the splice."},
 {id:"p24m4q5", q:"You must resolve two connectors 3 m apart in a data-center patch path, and separately characterize a 45 km span. How should you set the OTDR pulse width?", options:["Long pulses for both, for maximum dynamic range","Short pulses for both, since resolution always matters most","A long pulse for the patch path and a short pulse for the span","A short pulse for the patch path and a long pulse for the span"], answer:3, why:"Short pulses shrink the dead zone so nearby events separate, at the cost of range. Long pulses add dynamic range for long spans but blur events into a dead zone of meters to tens of meters. Match the pulse to the job instead of using one setting for both."}
],
"p24m5":[
 {id:"p24m5q1", q:"A GPON budget: OLT +3 dBm; 6 km feeder at 0.35 dB/km; 1:64 splitter at 21 dB; 2 km distribution; four connector pairs at 0.5 dB; ten splices at 0.1 dB. What does the ONT receive, and what margin remains against −27 dBm sensitivity?", options:["−20.3 dBm; 6.7 dB of margin","−23.8 dBm; 3.2 dB of margin","−29.8 dBm; it fails by 2.8 dB","−26.8 dBm; 0.2 dB of margin"], answer:1, why:"Loss is 2.1 + 21 + 0.7 + 2.0 + 1.0 = 26.8 dB, so +3 − 26.8 = −23.8 dBm, 3.2 dB above −27 dBm. Using a 1:32 splitter's 17.5 dB gives the optimistic −20.3 dBm, and −26.8 dBm forgets the launch power."},
 {id:"p24m5q2", q:"An operator moves a fully loaded 1:64 PON from GPON to XGS-PON. Roughly what is each home's worst-case downstream share before and after, and why is no new fiber needed?", options:["About 39 Mb/s to about 156 Mb/s; XGS-PON's 1577/1270 nm wavelengths coexist with GPON's","About 78 Mb/s to about 311 Mb/s; XGS-PON reuses GPON's 1490/1310 nm wavelengths","About 39 Mb/s to about 156 Mb/s; XGS-PON replaces the splitters with powered switches","About 2.5 Gb/s to about 10 Gb/s per home, since each ONT gets the full line rate"], answer:0, why:"2.488 Gb/s ÷ 64 ≈ 39 Mb/s and 9.953 Gb/s ÷ 64 ≈ 156 Mb/s. XGS-PON uses 1577 nm down and 1270 nm up, so it shares the passive splitters and fiber with GPON. 78 and 311 Mb/s are the 1:32 figures."},
 {id:"p24m5q3", q:"Two buildings 1.5 km apart need a 100 Gb/s link between QSFP28 ports, and only one duplex pair of single-mode fiber with LC connectors is available. Which optic fits?", options:["100GBASE-SR4: 850 nm over eight fibers on MPO-12","400GBASE-DR4: 1310 nm PAM4 over eight single-mode fibers on MPO-12, 500 m","100GBASE-LR4: four wavelengths near 1300 nm on LC duplex, 10 km","10GBASE-LR: 1310 nm on LC duplex, 10 km"], answer:2, why:"LR4 multiplexes four lanes onto one single-mode duplex pair with LC connectors and reaches 10 km. SR4 needs eight multimode fibers and reaches about 100 m; DR4 needs eight fibers and is a 400G optic; 10GBASE-LR has the reach but a tenth of the speed."},
 {id:"p24m5q4", q:"On a 10GBASE-LR link, ethtool -m shows receive power −11.5 dBm. It was −6.0 dBm at commissioning, the far end's transmit power is unchanged, and sensitivity is −14.4 dBm. What do you conclude?", options:["Normal optic aging; LR lasers and receivers lose several dB in their first years","The local receiver is degrading, so replace this SFP+ and retest the link","Chromatic dispersion has grown, so swap the optic for a 1550 nm ER part","About 5.5 dB of new path loss; inspect, clean and OTDR the route before it drops"], answer:3, why:"With the far transmitter steady, a 5.5 dB drop is new loss in the path: typically a dirty connector, a pinched slack loop or a degrading splice. Only 2.9 dB of margin remains before −14.4 dBm. The optic's diagnostics let you fix it before customers notice."},
 {id:"p24m5q5", q:"New 40GBASE-SR4 links over a 60 m OM4 MPO trunk pass fiber-by-fiber loss tests and clean inspection, yet the ports never come up. The trunk is Method B and the patch cords were ordered for Method A. Most likely problem?", options:["Polarity: mixing methods lands transmit lanes on transmit lanes","The OM4 trunk is too long for SR4","Base-8 optics cannot run on Base-12 trunks at all","The optics need a CWDM multiplexer to combine their four lanes"], answer:0, why:"Array polarity must land each transmitter on a receiver at the far end. TIA-568.3 Methods A, B and C each work on their own, but mixing a Method B trunk with Method A cords flips the lanes, so transmit meets transmit and no link forms. At 60 m the trunk is well inside SR4's 150 m OM4 reach."}
],
"p24m6":[
 {id:"p24m6q1", q:"The as-built marks fibers 13–24 as spare and dark. Before you cut and cleave them in an open closure, what do you do?", options:["Test each with a power meter; a reading above its floor means a source is on","Look at each end face with a direct-view eyepiece scope for a glow","Trust the as-built, since it is the document of record","Shine a VFL down each fiber; if no red light appears at the far end, the fiber is dark"], answer:0, why:"Telecom light is infrared and invisible, so neither your eye nor a red VFL can show that a fiber is dark, and records can be wrong. A power meter reading at its floor (around −60 dBm) is the evidence. Never look into a fiber with a direct-view scope."},
 {id:"p24m6q2", q:"A trench in soil is 4.5 ft deep, the spoil pile sits 3 ft back from the edge, and the nearest ladder is 35 ft from one worker. Which rule does the scene break?", options:["The spoil rule: spoil must sit at least 5 ft back from the edge","Neither: both the ladder rule and the protective-system rule start only at 5 ft deep","The protective-system rule: any trench over 4 ft needs a trench box","The ladder rule: at 4 ft or deeper a ladder must be within 25 ft of every worker"], answer:3, why:"Under OSHA Subpart P, a trench 4 ft or deeper needs a ladder within 25 ft of every worker, so 35 ft fails. The protective-system requirement starts at 5 ft, and spoil must be at least 2 ft back, which 3 ft satisfies."},
 {id:"p24m6q3", q:"While hand-digging toward your conduit you find a yellow mark and an orange mark crossing your path. What are they, and how do you proceed near them?", options:["Yellow is communications and orange is gas; machine-dig to 24 in, then hand-dig","Yellow is gas or oil and orange is communications; hand-dig inside the tolerance zone","Yellow is proposed excavation and orange is electric; stop and call 811 again","Yellow is gas or oil and orange is communications; a machine is fine 6 in away"], answer:1, why:"APWA colors: yellow is gas and oil, orange is communications, red is electric, white is proposed excavation. Within the 18–24 inch tolerance zone of any mark you expose the utility by hand, never with a machine. A gas strike is a fatality risk."},
 {id:"p24m6q4", q:"A DWDM amplifier output port reads +24 dBm. How should you treat it compared with a 0 dBm SFP?", options:["About 24 mW: Hazard Level 1M, safe unless viewed through a magnifier","About 2.4 mW: Hazard Level 1, safe for a brief accidental exposure","About 250 mW: Hazard Level 3B, able to injure eyes; disable it before opening","About 250 mW, but harmless to eyes because infrared does not trigger the blink reflex"], answer:2, why:"+24 dBm is 10^2.4 ≈ 251 mW, about 250 times a 0 dBm (1 mW) optic. Amplified lines at +20 to +30 dBm are Hazard Level 3B or 4. Invisible infrared is more dangerous, not less, precisely because the blink reflex never fires."},
 {id:"p24m6q5", q:"The four-gas meter reads O₂ 20.9%, LEL 0%, H₂S 0 ppm and CO 0 ppm at the vault, and the blower has run ten minutes. Once inside, a crew member wants to switch off the meter and blower to save battery. Your response?", options:["Keep both running: the space must be monitored and ventilated for the whole entry","Fine: a clean pre-entry test certifies the vault's atmosphere for the rest of the shift","Fine, as long as the attendant stays topside with the retrieval tripod","Switch the meter off, but retest right away if anyone smells gas"], answer:0, why:"Confined-space practice is to test before entry and continuously afterward, with a blower running, an attendant and a retrieval system. Gas can migrate in through ducts at any time, and carbon monoxide has no smell. A clean first reading certifies only that moment."}
],
"p24m7":[
 {id:"p24m7q1", q:"A fronthaul link has a 100 µs one-way latency budget, and the radio and DU equipment consume 30 µs of it. Using 4.9 µs per km of fiber, what is the longest fiber run allowed?", options:["About 20 km","About 7 km","About 1.4 km","About 14 km"], answer:3, why:"70 µs remains for the glass: 70 ÷ 4.9 ≈ 14.3 km. Using the full 100 µs (about 20 km) ignores the processing time, and halving it (about 7 km) wrongly treats a one-way budget as a round trip."},
 {id:"p24m7q2", q:"A business customer measures 40 ms round trip to a server reached over 2,400 km of fiber route and calls it 'too slow.' What is the propagation floor?", options:["About 23.5 ms round trip; the rest is routing, queuing and the server","About 11.8 ms round trip; the remaining 28 ms or so is network congestion","About 8 ms round trip, at the speed of light in vacuum","Near zero, because glass delay is negligible on land routes"], answer:0, why:"At about 4.9 µs per km, 2,400 km is 11.8 ms one way and 23.5 ms round trip before any router touches a packet. 11.8 ms is only the one-way figure, and light in glass travels at c ÷ n, about 204,000 km/s, not the vacuum speed."},
 {id:"p24m7q3", q:"A hospital wants its connection to survive any single fiber cut. Which design delivers that?", options:["A higher-count cable on the existing route","Two physically diverse routes into the building, as on a ring","Two fibers in the same cable, with one held in reserve as a hot spare","A DWDM system on the existing spur for more capacity"], answer:1, why:"A cut takes out everything in that cable or trench, so spares in the same sheath die together. Only route diversity, a ring or two separate paths, keeps service up after one cut: a cut ring stays up, a cut spur does not. Capacity does not add survivability."},
 {id:"p24m7q4", q:"Compare worst-case downstream shares: a DOCSIS 3.1 node sharing about 10 Gb/s among 400 homes, and an XGS-PON port shared by 32 homes. Which statement is right?", options:["Both give about 300 Mb/s per home, since each shares roughly 10 Gb/s","HFC gives each home 10 Gb/s, because DOCSIS 3.1 capacity is per subscriber","HFC about 25 Mb/s per home; XGS-PON about 311 Mb/s, and XGS-PON is symmetric","HFC about 25 Mb/s per home; XGS-PON about 78 Mb/s per home"], answer:2, why:"10,000 Mb/s ÷ 400 = 25 Mb/s; 9,953 Mb/s ÷ 32 ≈ 311 Mb/s, with equal upstream on XGS-PON against 1–2 Gb/s shared upstream on DOCSIS 3.1. 78 Mb/s is GPON's 1:32 share. Statistical multiplexing is why both still sell gigabit tiers."},
 {id:"p24m7q5", q:"A fiber tech with two years of documented as-builts wants to move into splicing and later QA. Which credential order matches the lesson's ladder?", options:["CFOT, then CFOS/S and CFOS/T with documented splicing and testing, then CFOS/O","CFOS/S first, since CFOT is only needed by engineers","RCDD first, since it is the entry credential for OSP work","Security+ first, then CFOT after the network and security fundamentals are learned"], answer:0, why:"The FOA path starts with CFOT; CFOS/S and CFOS/T by direct certification require CFOT plus documented splicing and testing experience, and CFOS/O fits the lead or QA rung. RCDD belongs to the design end of the ladder, years later."}
]
});
Object.assign(window.LQ,{
"p25m1":[
 {id:"p25m1q1", q:"A provisioning script draws a 10-character password uniformly from 36 symbols (a–z, 0–9) using a CSPRNG. About how much entropy does each password have?", options:["About 36 bits","About 80 bits","About 52 bits","About 360 bits"], answer:2, why:"Each symbol contributes log2(36) ≈ 5.17 bits, so ten give about 51.7 bits. Counting 8 bits per character (80) measures storage, not unpredictability, and 360 multiplies the alphabet size by the length instead of taking a logarithm."},
 {id:"p25m1q2", q:"Code review finds: random.seed(int(time.time())); key = random.getrandbits(256). An attacker knows which day the key was made. Roughly how many candidate keys must they try?", options:["About 86,400, one per second of that day","2^256, because the key is 256 bits long","2^128, because of the birthday bound","About 624, the outputs needed to clone the Mersenne Twister"], answer:0, why:"The key is a deterministic function of the seed, and a whole-second seed within one day has only 86,400 values. Key length is irrelevant when the generator supplies almost no entropy. The 624-output attack needs outputs the attacker does not have. Use secrets.token_bytes(32)."},
 {id:"p25m1q3", q:"An adversary can test 2^60 keys per second. How long would a full search of a 90-bit key space take?", options:["About 30 seconds","About 34 years","About 10 billion years","About 1,500 years"], answer:1, why:"2^90 ÷ 2^60 = 2^30 seconds ≈ 1.07 × 10^9 s ≈ 34 years (about 17 on average). Subtracting exponents gives 30, but that means 2^30 seconds, not 30. Each extra key bit doubles the time, which is why 128 bits is far out of reach."},
 {id:"p25m1q4", q:"A vendor pitches a proprietary, unpublished cipher 'so attackers have nothing to study.' Which response follows Kerckhoffs's principle?", options:["Accept it: hiding the algorithm adds a second secret layer on top of the key","Accept it if the key is 256 bits, which offsets any weakness in the unreviewed design","Reject it, because only the one-time pad has a proof of security behind it","Reject it: only the key should be secret, and hidden algorithms go unreviewed and leak"], answer:3, why:"Assume the enemy knows the system. A secret algorithm cannot be publicly reviewed and gets extracted from shipped products, as CSS, A5/1 and Crypto-1 were. A long key cannot rescue a structurally weak design. Choose published, heavily attacked algorithms such as AES."},
 {id:"p25m1q5", q:"A laptop has full-disk encryption with a strong, properly generated key. Which threat does it NOT address?", options:["A thief who steals the laptop while it is powered off","A drive pulled out and read in another computer","Malware running while the user is logged in","A discarded drive recovered from a recycler"], answer:2, why:"Full-disk encryption protects data at rest from someone holding the hardware. Once the user logs in the volume is unlocked, so malware reads plaintext like any other program. Cryptography does not protect secrets from an adversary who already controls the endpoint."}
],
"p25m2":[
 {id:"p25m2q1", q:"A service encrypts 10 million messages a day under one AES-GCM key with random 96-bit nonces and follows NIST's 2^32-message cap. Roughly how long until it must rotate the key?", options:["About 4.3 days","About 14 months (about 429 days)","Never, since random 96-bit nonces cannot collide in practice","About 43 days"], answer:1, why:"2^32 ≈ 4.29 × 10^9 messages ÷ 10^7 per day ≈ 429 days. The cap exists because random nonces collide with probability about n²/2^97, and a single GCM repeat leaks plaintext XORs and lets an attacker forge tags. Counter nonces or XChaCha20 avoid the limit."},
 {id:"p25m2q2", q:"You must encrypt disk sectors in place: random access is required, and the ciphertext must be exactly the sector size. Which mode fits?", options:["AES-GCM with a random nonce per sector","AES-ECB, since each sector is independent","AES-CBC with the IV stored in the sector","AES-XTS with the sector number as the tweak"], answer:3, why:"XTS is the tweaked mode built for sectors: no extra space, random access, and the sector number makes identical data encrypt differently in different places. GCM needs room for a nonce and tag, CBC needs an IV and padding, and ECB leaks repeated blocks."},
 {id:"p25m2q3", q:"Telemetry flows from an ARM board without AES instructions to an x86 server with AES-NI. Which choice is the better fit for the board?", options:["ChaCha20-Poly1305: fast and constant-time in plain software","AES-GCM, because AES is always the faster cipher","AES-CBC, because it avoids nonce management","AES-CTR, because it saves the 16-byte tag on a slow device"], answer:0, why:"Without hardware AES, software AES is slow and its table lookups can leak timing. ChaCha20-Poly1305 uses only additions, rotations and XORs, runs fast everywhere and is an AEAD, and the server handles it easily too. CBC and CTR alone give no integrity."},
 {id:"p25m2q4", q:"The message 'PAY $0100 TO ACCT 42' is encrypted with AES-CTR and no MAC. An attacker who knows the format XORs a chosen value into the ciphertext bytes over the amount. What happens on decryption?", options:["Decryption fails because the keystream no longer matches","The whole message decrypts to garbage, so the change is noticed","The amount becomes the attacker's chosen value, with no error","Only the block containing the change is garbled, as in CBC"], answer:2, why:"CTR XORs a keystream, so flipping ciphertext bits flips exactly the same plaintext bits; knowing the plaintext, the attacker can set the amount, and nothing detects it. An AEAD such as AES-GCM rejects any modified ciphertext before releasing plaintext."},
 {id:"p25m2q5", q:"A homegrown protocol decrypts AES-CBC, parses the plaintext, and only then checks an HMAC. Parse failures return 'bad format' and tag failures return 'bad MAC.' What is the right fix?", options:["Replace PKCS#7 with a cleverer padding scheme that leaks less","Check the MAC first (or use an AEAD) and return one identical error","Use a longer, unpredictable IV for every message sent","Encrypt the error messages so an attacker cannot read which one came back"], answer:1, why:"Acting on unauthenticated plaintext turns distinguishable errors into an oracle, the padding-oracle pattern. Encrypt-then-MAC with the tag checked first, or an AEAD, rejects every tampered ciphertext the same way before any decryption. Better padding or IVs leave the oracle in place."}
],
"p25m3":[
 {id:"p25m3q1", q:"A system names stored files by the first 64 bits of their SHA-256 hash. Roughly how many files can it hold before an accidental collision becomes likely?", options:["About 2^63","About 2^64","About 2^16","About 2^32"], answer:3, why:"Collisions follow the birthday bound, about 1.18 × √N draws, and √(2^64) = 2^32 ≈ 4.3 billion. 2^64 is the cost of matching one specific digest (a preimage), not of any two files matching. Truncating a hash halves its collision resistance in bits."},
 {id:"p25m3q2", q:"An API checks request signatures with `if tag == expected:` in a language whose string comparison stops at the first differing byte. What is wrong?", options:["Nothing: a 256-bit HMAC tag is far too long for anyone to guess","Timing leaks how many leading bytes matched, so a tag can be found byte by byte","The comparison should be case-insensitive for hex-encoded tags","HMAC tags should be compared only after the request body has been fully decrypted"], answer:1, why:"An early-exit comparison takes longer the more leading bytes match, so repeated requests reveal a valid tag one byte at a time. Use a constant-time comparison such as hmac.compare_digest. The HMAC construction is fine; the comparison is what leaks."},
 {id:"p25m3q3", q:"A leaked user table shows 1,200 accounts sharing exactly the same password hash value. What does that reveal about how the passwords were stored?", options:["There was no per-user salt","The hash function has a collision weakness","The pepper was the same for every user","The Argon2id memory cost was set too low"], answer:0, why:"With a unique random salt per user, identical passwords produce different hashes. Equal hashes mean no salt, so one guess cracks all 1,200 at once and precomputed tables apply. A shared pepper is normal and would not make salted hashes match."},
 {id:"p25m3q4", q:"An attacker has 94^6 ≈ 6.9 × 10^11 password candidates. How long to try them all against unsalted SHA-256 at 10^10 guesses per second, versus Argon2id (64 MiB) at 2,000 guesses per second?", options:["About 7 seconds versus about 1.1 years","About 11 minutes versus about 110 years","About 69 seconds versus about 40 days","About 69 seconds versus about 11 years"], answer:3, why:"6.9 × 10^11 ÷ 10^10 ≈ 69 s; 6.9 × 10^11 ÷ 2,000 ≈ 3.45 × 10^8 s ≈ 11 years. The memory-hard hash slows each guess about five million times, turning a minute into a decade. Speed is exactly why plain SHA-256 is a poor password hash."},
 {id:"p25m3q5", q:"A Merkle tree commits to 1,000,000 SHA-256 leaves. About how large is the proof that one particular leaf is included?", options:["About 1,000 hashes, roughly 32 KB","Exactly one hash: the 32-byte root","About 20 hashes, roughly 640 bytes","About 500,000 hashes, half the tree"], answer:2, why:"A proof supplies one sibling hash per level: log2(1,000,000) ≈ 20 levels × 32 bytes ≈ 640 bytes. The root alone says nothing about a particular leaf, and proof size grows logarithmically, not with the square root or half the leaves."}
],
"p25m4":[
 {id:"p25m4q1", q:"Toy RSA: p = 11, q = 17, e = 7. What is the private exponent d?", options:["107","15","23","133"], answer:2, why:"n = 187 and φ(n) = 10 × 16 = 160, so d = 7⁻¹ mod 160 = 23, since 7 × 23 = 161 = 160 + 1. 107 is the inverse modulo n rather than φ(n). Computing φ(n) needs p and q, which is why factoring n breaks RSA."},
 {id:"p25m4q2", q:"Diffie–Hellman with public p = 29 and g = 2. Alice's secret is a = 4 and Bob's is b = 9. What shared secret do both compute?", options:["24","14","16","19"], answer:0, why:"A = 2^4 mod 29 = 16 and B = 2^9 mod 29 = 19. Alice computes 19^4 mod 29 = 24 and Bob 16^9 mod 29 = 24, both g^(ab). 14 is A × B mod 29 = g^(a+b), which any eavesdropper could compute, and 16 and 19 are the public values."},
 {id:"p25m4q3", q:"A design encrypts data with AES-256 and wraps the AES key with RSA-2048. About what security level does the whole system offer?", options:["256 bits, set by AES","About 112 bits, set by RSA-2048","About 184 bits, the average of the two","2048 bits, set by the modulus"], answer:1, why:"An attacker breaks the weakest link: recovering the wrapped key through RSA-2048 costs about 2^112 under NIST SP 800-57, so the AES-256 margin is wasted. Balance a row instead: AES-128 with P-256 or RSA-3072, or AES-256 with P-521."},
 {id:"p25m4q4", q:"A server decrypts raw 'textbook' RSA with no padding. An attacker intercepts c and submits c' = c × 3^e mod n. What does c' decrypt to?", options:["m, because the extra factor is stripped during decryption","m³ mod n","A random-looking value, since RSA scrambles its input","3m mod n, because textbook RSA is malleable"], answer:3, why:"(c × 3^e)^d = m^(ed) × 3^(ed) ≡ 3m (mod n), so the attacker predictably transforms a plaintext without the key. That malleability, along with determinism, is why real RSA uses OAEP for encryption and PSS for signatures."},
 {id:"p25m4q5", q:"Two devices run ephemeral X25519 with no signatures or certificates, and a router between them can modify traffic. What can the router do?", options:["Nothing: the discrete-log problem protects the shared secret from anyone on the path","Only record the traffic for a future quantum attack","Run a separate exchange with each device and read or alter everything in between","Only learn the public values, which reveal nothing useful"], answer:2, why:"Diffie–Hellman authenticates no one, so an active attacker completes one exchange with each side and relays traffic, decrypting and re-encrypting it. The discrete-log problem only stops a passive eavesdropper. Signatures or certificates over the exchange defeat the man in the middle."}
],
"p25m5":[
 {id:"p25m5q1", q:"A production Python client calls requests.get(url, verify=False). What does an attacker on the network path gain?", options:["Nothing: the traffic is still encrypted with AES-GCM end to end, whoever is listening","Only the server name, which SNI already sends in the clear","Only the ability to present a revoked but otherwise valid certificate","Full man-in-the-middle: any certificate passes, so they read and alter traffic"], answer:3, why:"Encryption to an unverified party is encryption to the attacker. With verification off, the client accepts the attacker's certificate, completes TLS with them, and they relay to the real server. Disabled verification shipped to production is the most common application-level TLS bug."},
 {id:"p25m5q2", q:"After a teammate rebuilds a server, ssh prints REMOTE HOST IDENTIFICATION HAS CHANGED. What is the correct response?", options:["Confirm the new fingerprint out of band, such as via the console, first","Delete the known_hosts line and reconnect, since rebuilds always change keys","Set StrictHostKeyChecking=no for that host so the warning stops appearing","Switch to password authentication until the new host key has settled"], answer:0, why:"The warning is SSH's man-in-the-middle alarm. A rebuild is a likely cause, but so is an attacker. Confirm the fingerprint out of band and only then update known_hosts, or use an SSH CA that signs host keys. Deleting the line blindly accepts whatever answers."},
 {id:"p25m5q3", q:"An attacker records TLS 1.3 sessions in 2026 and steals the server's certificate private key in 2027, with no quantum computer. Can they decrypt the recorded sessions?", options:["Yes: the private key decrypts the premaster secret sent in each recorded session","No: keys came from ephemeral exchange; the certificate key only signed the handshake","Yes, but only the sessions that negotiated AES-128 rather than AES-256","Yes, because the Finished HMAC and record keys derive from the certificate key"], answer:1, why:"TLS 1.3 removed RSA key transport and requires ephemeral (EC)DHE, so the private key only produced the CertificateVerify signature. Without the deleted ephemeral secrets the recorded key shares stay locked: forward secrecy. Decrypting a premaster secret with the server key was TLS 1.2's RSA mode."},
 {id:"p25m5q4", q:"An audit finds an Active Directory service account with an SPN, RC4 allowed and a 9-character password set in 2014. What is the main risk, and the fix?", options:["Golden tickets: the account can mint any ticket, so rotate the krbtgt key twice","Clock skew: old accounts drift out of tolerance, so synchronize NTP on all DCs","Kerberoasting: its tickets can be cracked offline; use AES and a long random password or gMSA","Pass-the-ticket: stolen tickets stay valid for ten hours, so cut every ticket lifetime to one hour"], answer:2, why:"Any domain user can request a service ticket encrypted under the account's key and guess the weak password offline, never touching the server. Long random passwords or group-managed service accounts with AES-only encryption types defeat it. Golden tickets concern the krbtgt key, not service accounts."},
 {id:"p25m5q5", q:"Users click through a self-signed certificate warning on an internal dashboard, and the team plans to renew certificates by hand once a year. What should replace this?", options:["An ACME CA (private, like step-ca, or Let's Encrypt via dns-01) with auto-renewal","A two-year commercial certificate, to cut down on renewals","Turning TLS off internally, since the dashboard already sits behind the corporate firewall","Pinning the self-signed certificate in every user's browser"], answer:0, why:"Click-through warnings train users to ignore real attacks. ACME issues trusted certificates and renews them automatically, which is now mandatory: SC-081 caps lifetimes at 200 days from March 2026, 100 from 2027 and 47 from 2029, so two-year certificates no longer exist."}
],
"p25m6":[
 {id:"p25m6q1", q:"Customer records must stay secret for X = 12 years, your migration will take Y = 4 years, and you estimate a cryptographically relevant quantum computer in Z = 14 years. What does Mosca's inequality say?", options:["X + Y = 16 > 14: data encrypted now will be exposed; start on key exchange now","X = 12 < 14: the data's secrecy expires before the machine arrives, so no action is needed","Y = 4 < 14: there is ample time, so begin in about ten years","Only signatures are at risk, so the inequality does not apply"], answer:0, why:"Exposure happens when secrecy lifetime plus migration time exceeds the time to a quantum computer: 12 + 4 = 16 > 14. Traffic harvested during the migration years still needs twelve years of secrecy. Comparing X or Y alone with Z ignores that the two periods add."},
 {id:"p25m6q2", q:"Your crypto inventory lists: (a) AES-256-GCM backups, (b) X25519 VPN key exchange, (c) Ed25519 firmware signatures, (d) Argon2id password hashes. Which must be replaced for the quantum era?", options:["All four, since a quantum computer halves the strength of every algorithm","Only (a) and (d), because Grover's search halves their effective key bits","Only (b) and (c), because Shor breaks elliptic-curve key exchange and signatures","Only (b), since signatures never need migrating before the machine exists"], answer:2, why:"Shor solves elliptic-curve discrete logs, so X25519 and Ed25519 fall completely and need ML-KEM and ML-DSA (or SLH-DSA or LMS). Grover only square-roots brute force: AES-256 keeps about 128 bits and hash-based schemes like Argon2id survive. Firmware signatures must migrate early."},
 {id:"p25m6q3", q:"Why does a hybrid TLS group such as X25519MLKEM768 derive the session key from both secrets rather than using ML-KEM alone?", options:["ML-KEM's 32-byte shared secret is too short to key AES-256 on its own","It stays safe if either survives, a hedge after SIKE fell to a classical attack","X25519 supplies the server authentication that a KEM such as ML-KEM cannot provide","Browsers reject any key share over 1 KB unless it is paired with X25519"], answer:1, why:"HKDF over both secrets means an attacker must break X25519 and ML-KEM. Lattice schemes are young, and competition candidates like SIKE were broken on a laptop, so keeping the proven classical exchange hedges that risk. Neither key exchange authenticates anyone; signatures do that."},
 {id:"p25m6q4", q:"With budget for one post-quantum project this year, which should go first?", options:["Re-signing the public website's whole certificate chain with ML-DSA this year","Upgrading session-cache encryption from AES-128 to AES-256","Replacing Ed25519 SSH user keys on admin laptops","Hybrid key exchange on the VPN carrying 25-year-secret records"], answer:3, why:"Recorded key exchanges can be broken retroactively, so confidentiality of long-lived data comes first. Forging a signature needs the quantum computer at signing time, so web certificates and SSH user keys can follow later, and NIST still treats AES-128 as acceptable."},
 {id:"p25m6q5", q:"New ONTs ship in 2027 with a 15-year field life, and their bootloader holds a single Ed25519 verification key. What should change before they ship?", options:["Nothing: signature forgery needs the quantum computer at signing time, so firmware can wait","Reserve a second key slot (ML-DSA-65 or LMS) so a signed update can switch algorithms","Move to RSA-4096 signatures, since larger keys resist Shor","Encrypt the firmware with AES-256 instead of signing it"], answer:1, why:"These devices will still verify updates after 2035, when a quantum attacker could forge Ed25519 signatures at that moment, and a single-key bootloader has no path to a new algorithm. Room for an ML-DSA or LMS key gives crypto agility. RSA of any size falls to Shor."}
],
"p25m7":[
 {id:"p25m7q1", q:"A SaaS has 10,000 tenants, each with its own DEK wrapped by one environment KEK. What does rotating the KEK involve?", options:["Re-encrypting every tenant's data under the new KEK","Re-wrapping the 10,000 DEKs (about 320 KB); the data itself is untouched","Generating new DEKs for every tenant and re-encrypting all of the stored data","Nothing, because KEKs never need rotation once they are inside the KMS"], answer:1, why:"In envelope encryption the KEK protects only the DEKs, so rotation unwraps and re-wraps 10,000 × 32 bytes and no bulk data moves. That is the design's main operational payoff. Keeping KEKs inside a KMS limits exposure but does not remove cryptoperiods."},
 {id:"p25m7q2", q:"A departing tenant asks for deletion, but its data sits in seven years of encrypted backups. With per-tenant DEKs, how do you make that data unrecoverable?", options:["Destroy the KMS root key that sits above every KEK","Rotate the environment KEK that wraps the tenant's DEK","Re-encrypt that tenant's backups under a brand-new DEK","Destroy that tenant's DEK, and every backup copy of it"], answer:3, why:"Crypto-shredding: without its 32-byte DEK, the tenant's ciphertext in every backup is permanently unreadable, and no other tenant is affected. Destroying the root key would wipe out everyone, and rotating the KEK simply re-wraps the same DEK, so the data stays recoverable."},
 {id:"p25m7q3", q:"A database uses provider-managed encryption at rest (SSE-KMS). An attacker uses SQL injection to dump the customers table. Does the at-rest encryption help?", options:["No: queries return plaintext; at-rest encryption only defeats stolen disks and media","Yes: the dump arrives as ciphertext, since the storage layer underneath is encrypted","Yes, but only if the KMS key was rotated within the last month","Partly: indexed columns stay encrypted, though other columns leak"], answer:0, why:"Storage encryption is transparent to the database engine, so every query, injected or not, returns plaintext. It stops a thief holding drives, snapshots or backup tapes. Against an application compromise you need to fix the injection and use application-level encryption with keys the database does not hold."},
 {id:"p25m7q4", q:"An attacker gains a shell on the code-signing server, which is authorized to call the HSM. The private key was never exported. What is the remaining risk, and the key control?", options:["None: a key that never leaves the HSM cannot be used by an intruder","Only a denial of service, by flooding the HSM with requests","They can sign malware via the HSM; log, rate-limit and alert on every signing","They can extract the private key in plaintext through PKCS#11 export calls"], answer:2, why:"An HSM prevents key extraction, not key use: any process authorized to call it can sign. Usage logging, rate limits, alerts and M-of-N or per-request approvals are what catch abuse. A correctly configured HSM refuses plaintext export of private keys."},
 {id:"p25m7q5", q:"A GitHub Actions workflow stores a long-lived AWS access key as a repository secret and uses third-party actions referenced by tag. What is the best improvement?", options:["Base64-encode the key before storing it as a repository secret","Use OIDC for short-lived role credentials, and pin actions to commit hashes","Move the key into an environment variable in the workflow file","Keep the long-lived key but rotate it every five years on a schedule"], answer:1, why:"With OIDC federation the pipeline trades a signed identity token for credentials valid for minutes, so nothing long-lived can leak. Pinning actions to commit hashes stops a retagged action from exfiltrating secrets. Base64 is encoding, not encryption, and a key in the workflow file lives in git."}
]
});
Object.assign(window.LQ,{
"p26m1":[
 {id:"p26m1q1", q:"In March, 860 P3 tickets came due and 807 were resolved within target. What is the SLA attainment, and how does it compare with the 90–97% range where most desks live?", options:["6.2%, well below the typical range","88.4%, just below the typical range","93.8%, inside the typical range","93.8%, above the typical range, so the target is too loose"], answer:2, why:"Attainment = resolved within target ÷ tickets due × 100 = 807 ÷ 860 × 100 ≈ 93.8%, inside the usual 90–97% band. 6.2% is the breach rate, not attainment, and 88.4% comes from dividing by 913, which counts the 53 breached tickets twice."},
 {id:"p26m1q2", q:"The CFO, calm and polite, cannot open the finance application. There is no workaround and month-end close is due tomorrow. Using the lesson's priority table, what priority is this?", options:["P1 Critical, because the requester is an executive","P2 High: a key person is blocked","P3 Medium, because only one user is affected","P4 Low, because the CFO is not upset"], answer:1, why:"Priority comes from impact and urgency, not rank or mood. One key person blocked with a deadline matches P2 High; P1 is for a whole site or critical service down. Calling it P3 ignores that there is no workaround and the damage grows by tomorrow."},
 {id:"p26m1q3", q:"Label these records: (a) a new hire requests VPN access; (b) the VPN has been down for everyone since 09:00; (c) an investigation into why the VPN concentrator crashes every Monday; (d) a scheduled concentrator firmware upgrade on Saturday.", options:["(a) incident, (b) problem, (c) change, (d) service request","(a) service request, (b) problem, (c) incident, (d) change","(a) change, (b) incident, (c) known error, (d) service request","(a) service request, (b) incident, (c) problem, (d) change"], answer:3, why:"Access for a new hire is a normal-delivery service request; an outage is an incident to restore; hunting the cause of recurring crashes is problem management; altering the device is a change. Calling the outage a problem confuses restoring service with finding root cause."},
 {id:"p26m1q4", q:"A P2 ticket opens Friday at 4:30 p.m. Business hours are 8:00 a.m. to 5:00 p.m., Monday to Friday, and the P2 resolution target is 8 business hours. When is it due?", options:["Monday 3:30 p.m.","Saturday 12:30 a.m.","Monday 4:00 p.m.","Monday 4:30 p.m."], answer:0, why:"SLA clocks count only agreed hours. Friday 4:30–5:00 uses 0.5 hours, and the remaining 7.5 run from Monday 8:00 a.m. to 3:30 p.m. Saturday 12:30 a.m. counts clock hours, and Monday 4:00 p.m. forgets the half hour already used on Friday."},
 {id:"p26m1q5", q:"Before replacing core switch SW-03, you need to know which services will be affected while it is down. Where should you look?", options:["The ITAM record, for SW-03's warranty and owner","The CMDB, for configuration items and services related to SW-03","The SLA table, for the priorities of the affected services","The on-call schedule, for who is paged if SW-03 fails"], answer:1, why:"The CMDB stores configuration items and their relationships, which is exactly what answers 'what dies if this dies?' ITAM tracks the money side: purchase, warranty, owner and disposal. The impact analysis then feeds the change record for the replacement."}
],
"p26m2":[
 {id:"p26m2q1", q:"A connectivity path has 16 links in series and one has failed. Using half-split, how many tests does it take in the worst case to find the bad link?", options:["8","16","4","2"], answer:2, why:"Each test halves the suspect range: 16 → 8 → 4 → 2 → 1, so log2(16) = 4 tests. Testing link by link could take 16, and 8 corresponds to one split followed by a linear search of the remaining half."},
 {id:"p26m2q2", q:"A tech gathers information, forms a theory, confirms it, reinstalls the print driver and writes up the ticket. The next morning the user reports that a second application still cannot print. Which step was skipped?", options:["Verify full system functionality and implement preventive measures","Identify the problem","Establish a plan of action and implement the solution, following vendor guidance","Document findings, actions and outcomes"], answer:0, why:"Step 5 requires confirming the whole system works, with the user and across the affected functions, before documenting and closing. The tech jumped from the fix to documentation, so the user found the remaining failure instead of the technician."},
 {id:"p26m2q3", q:"Within ten minutes, Event ID 4740 appears on the domain controller for 15 different users, all naming the same unfamiliar caller computer. What is the right move?", options:["Unlock each account and tell the users to update the saved passwords on their phones","Raise the lockout threshold so users stop being locked out","Reset all 15 passwords and close the tickets","Escalate to security as likely password spraying, with the event details"], answer:3, why:"One user locking out repeatedly usually means a stale cached password; many accounts failing from one unknown host is an attack pattern. The 4740 events name the source, which is evidence for the security team. Unlocking or raising thresholds only helps the attacker keep guessing."},
 {id:"p26m2q4", q:"A server's System log shows Event ID 41 at 02:13 and Event ID 6008 at 02:15. No updates were installed and nobody remembers a reboot. What does that tell you?", options:["A service failed to start after a planned restart","The machine lost power or crashed instead of shutting down cleanly","Someone failed to log on repeatedly overnight","Windows restarted cleanly on its own schedule to finish installing an update"], answer:1, why:"Event 41 records an unexpected shutdown and 6008 a dirty previous shutdown, so look at power, hardware and crash dumps. Service start failures are 7000/7001 and failed logons are 4625. A clean update restart does not log these events."},
 {id:"p26m2q5", q:"Which escalation note follows the lesson's guidance?", options:["User says the VPN doesn't work at home. Tried a few things, nothing helped. Escalating to L2 for a look.","VPN broken for lots of users since this morning, very urgent, everyone is waiting on this, please fix ASAP!","VPN error 809 for 6 remote users since 07:40; office Wi-Fi fine; reinstall failed; logs attached; suspect last night's gateway change.","Probably the firewall again like last month, since it was the firewall then too. Can network take a look when they get a free minute today?"], answer:2, why:"A good escalation gives symptom, scope and start time, what was tested with results, evidence and a hypothesis, so the next tier starts where you stopped. Vague or emotional notes get bounced back, and a bare guess without tests gives the next tier nothing to verify."}
],
"p26m3":[
 {id:"p26m3q1", q:"On an Ubuntu server, writes fail with 'No space left on device,' but df -h shows / at 61% used. What should you check next?", options:["df -i, because the filesystem may have run out of inodes","sfc /scannow, to repair corrupted system files on the disk","free -h, because swap may be full and blocking writes","Nothing more; a reboot will clear the cached disk usage"], answer:0, why:"Free blocks plus failed writes points to inode exhaustion, often millions of tiny files in a log or cache directory. df -i shows inode use and du helps find the culprit. sfc is a Windows tool, and swap has nothing to do with filesystem space."},
 {id:"p26m3q2", q:"An employee moves from Finance to Sales. How should their access change?", options:["Add the Sales groups and keep Finance access for a smooth transition","Copy the permissions of a current Sales employee onto the account","Remove the Finance groups and add the Sales groups","Create a new account and delete the old one"], answer:2, why:"In joiner–mover–leaver, access lives in groups, so a mover is a group swap. Keeping old groups causes privilege creep against least privilege, and copying a colleague's permissions inherits whatever extra rights they accumulated. A new account breaks history for no gain."},
 {id:"p26m3q3", q:"A caller says she is the VP of Sales, traveling, has lost her phone and needs her MFA reset immediately. The number shown is unknown to you. What do you do?", options:["Reset it at once, since a VP's urgent request outranks the procedure","Ask her to read back her current password to prove who she is","Reset it now, then email her work address afterward to confirm it really was her","Verify first: call back on the directory number or confirm with her manager"], answer:3, why:"Help-desk resets are a favorite social-engineering target; the 2023 casino breaches began with such a call. Verification must use a channel the caller does not control, such as a directory callback or manager confirmation. Never ask for a password, and urgency or rank is not proof."},
 {id:"p26m3q4", q:"After a CMOS battery failure, a laptop's clock shows a date three years in the past. The user cannot sign in to the domain, and HTTPS sites show certificate errors. What is the first fix?", options:["Resynchronize the clock via NTP, since Kerberos tolerates only 5 minutes of skew","Reset the user's password, because the cached credential expired","Reinstall the trusted root certificates, since the HTTPS errors point to a bad store","Rejoin the laptop to the domain"], answer:0, why:"Kerberos rejects requests more than five minutes off, and certificates look 'not yet valid' when the local date precedes their issue date, so one cause explains both symptoms. Fix the time (and the battery); rejoining the domain or resetting the password would not help."},
 {id:"p26m3q5", q:"After a firmware update, a provisioned laptop asks for its 48-digit BitLocker recovery key, and the desk cannot find it in Entra ID or AD. Which provisioning step was skipped, and what follows?", options:["TPM enablement; turn BitLocker off in the firmware settings so the laptop boots","Recovery-key escrow; the data is unrecoverable, so reimage and restore from backup","LAPS; sign in with the managed local admin password to bypass the recovery prompt","Asset tagging; the key is stored on the CMDB record created at provisioning"], answer:1, why:"The checklist requires escrowing recovery keys because firmware changes alter TPM measurements and trigger recovery. Without the key the encrypted volume is unreadable by design. A local admin password cannot unlock BitLocker, and the CMDB holds asset data, not keys."}
],
"p26m4":[
 {id:"p26m4q1", q:"You are writing a KB article about Outlook repeatedly asking for a password after a password change. Which title follows the house style?", options:["Outlook authentication and credential prompts: background and overview","Outlook keeps asking for password after a password change","KB-0412 Outlook issue (see INC-88213)","How to simply fix Outlook prompts"], answer:1, why:"The title should be the phrase a technician would actually search, describing the symptom in users' words. Ticket numbers and overviews are unsearchable, and the style guide bans 'simply,' which tells a struggling reader the problem is their fault."},
 {id:"p26m4q2", q:"Which numbered step follows the documentation style guide?", options:["1. Event Viewer should be opened, and then simply filter the logs.","1. Open EV and filter the SL for the relevant ID.","1. Open Event Viewer, filter for 4740, unlock the account and update the phone.","1. Open Event Viewer and select Windows Logs > Security."], answer:3, why:"Good steps start with a verb, use the active voice, do one action and expand acronyms. The first is passive and says 'simply'; the second uses unexplained acronyms; the third crams four actions into one step, so a reader who fails midway cannot tell where."},
 {id:"p26m4q3", q:"You need a drawing that shows which VLANs ride which trunks between three switches. What kind of diagram is it?", options:["Layer 2: switches, trunks and VLAN IDs labeled on the links","Physical: rack elevations and patch-panel port numbers","Layer 3: subnets, gateways and firewalls","One combined drawing showing racks, VLANs and subnets together"], answer:0, why:"VLANs and trunks are layer 2 concepts, so a layer 2 diagram with VLAN IDs on the trunk lines answers the question. The lesson's rule is one layer per drawing; combining layers makes every question harder to answer, and physical or layer 3 views omit trunk membership."},
 {id:"p26m4q4", q:"A draft runbook includes the backup server's admin password 'so on-call doesn't have to hunt for it.' What should you do?", options:["Keep it, but bold it so it is easy to find at 2 a.m.","Move it into a screenshot so text search cannot find it","Replace it with a link to the password vault entry","Keep it, since the wiki already requires a login"], answer:2, why:"Documents get copied, exported, cached and read by many people, so a password written in one is a password spent. The style guide says to link to the vault entry, which keeps access controlled and logged. A screenshot still exposes it and goes stale."},
 {id:"p26m4q5", q:"While working a ticket you follow KB step 3 and find it is wrong for the new interface. Under Knowledge-Centered Service, what should you do?", options:["Note it in your own notes and keep using your workaround","Improve or flag the article now, as part of resolving the ticket","Write a new, corrected article so the old one stays intact as history","Wait for the article's scheduled review date"], answer:1, why:"KCS captures and improves knowledge in the flow of work: when reuse reveals an error, whoever found it fixes or flags it immediately. A duplicate article splits search results, and waiting months for the review leaves every other technician following a wrong step."}
],
"p26m5":[
 {id:"p26m5q1", q:"Your host has 32 GB of RAM and you reserve 6 GB for the host OS. Planned guests: a DC at 4 GB, two Windows 11 clients at 4 GB each, OPNsense at 1 GB, Ubuntu/Docker at 2 GB and Wazuh at 8 GB. Does it fit?", options:["No: 27 GB of guests plus the host exceeds 32 GB","Yes: guests only use the RAM they actually need, so the totals do not matter","No: each VM needs about twice its assigned RAM","Yes: 23 GB of guests plus 6 GB for the host is 29 GB, leaving about 3 GB"], answer:3, why:"4 + 4 + 4 + 1 + 2 + 8 = 23 GB of guests, plus 6 GB for the host, is 29 GB of 32. It fits, but tightly, so start the SIEM only when you need it. Assuming guests 'use only what they need' leads to swapping and a lab that crawls."},
 {id:"p26m5q2", q:"Which résumé bullet follows the action verb + what + how + measurable result pattern?", options:["Responsible for splicing and testing duties on assigned fiber segments","Spliced and OTDR-tested 2,400+ fibers against loss budgets; zero rework across 18 segments","Skilled in splicing, OTDR testing and documentation","Worked daily with OTDRs, power meters, fusion splicers and as-built reports on fiber crews"], answer:1, why:"The strong bullet names the action, the work, the method and a number a recruiter can weigh. 'Responsible for' describes a duty, not an accomplishment, and skills or tool lists show exposure without evidence of results."},
 {id:"p26m5q3", q:"A posting asks for 'Microsoft Intune' and 'ServiceNow,' and your résumé says 'MDM tools' and 'ticketing software.' What should you change?", options:["Nothing, because recruiters know these mean the same things","Add a skills graphic in a sidebar so the terms stand out","Mirror the posting's exact terms wherever they are true for you","Add the terms in white text so only the ATS sees them"], answer:2, why:"ATS filters match keywords literally, so 'MDM tools' may never match 'Microsoft Intune.' Use the exact terms where you have the experience. Sidebars and graphics break ATS parsing, and hidden keyword stuffing is dishonest and obvious to the human who reads it next."},
 {id:"p26m5q4", q:"Year-one plan at the lesson's 2026 prices: Google IT Support for 5 months at $49 a month, both A+ cores, Network+ and Security+. What is the total exam and course spend?", options:["$1,357","$1,582","$2,321","$1,631"], answer:3, why:"5 × $49 = $245; A+ is two exams at $274 = $548; Network+ $399; Security+ $439; total $1,631. $1,357 counts A+ as a single exam, $1,582 is the lesson's four-month version, and $2,321 adds ITIL Foundation at $690."},
 {id:"p26m5q5", q:"After Security+, you lean toward networking and want a network engineer role. What is the next rung on the lesson's ladder?", options:["CCNA, because the middle rung is a choice and CCNA fits a network lean","Both CCNA and Linux+, finished before applying for any new role","OSCP+, skipping the middle rungs to reach a higher pay band sooner","GREM, since it carries the highest pay band on the whole ladder"], answer:0, why:"Rung 4 is Linux+ or CCNA, not both: CCNA for network work, Linux+ for systems and security. Jumping to OSCP+ or GREM skips the administration depth those exams assume, and stacking both middle certifications delays applying without improving your odds."}
],
"p26m6":[
 {id:"p26m6q1", q:"A service desk posting went up five days ago, and you meet 6 of its 10 listed requirements. What does the lesson advise?", options:["Wait until you meet every requirement, then apply","Apply now, tailoring the résumé's top third to the posting's exact words","Send your general résumé to many similar postings instead, to maximize volume","Skip it, since after 48 hours a posting is effectively closed"], answer:1, why:"Requirement lists are wish lists, and about 60% plus evidence of learning earns interviews for support roles. Applying quickly helps, but five days is not too late. Ten to fifteen tailored applications a week beat fifty identical ones."},
 {id:"p26m6q2", q:"In a phone screen for a help-desk role you are asked how Kerberos constrained delegation works, and you do not know. What is the strongest answer?", options:["Give a confident general answer about delegation, then move on before any follow-up","Explain that the topic is outside the scope of a help-desk role","Steer the conversation toward a related topic you know well instead","Admit it, then explain how you'd find out: KB, vendor docs, time-box, escalate"], answer:3, why:"Interviewers for support roles are testing method and honesty, and 'I'd check the KB and docs, time-box it and escalate with these facts' is a correct tier-one answer. A bluff is easy for a technical interviewer to spot and casts doubt on all your other answers."},
 {id:"p26m6q3", q:"A candidate answers a behavioral question: 'We had a big outage once. The team worked hard and fixed it. It went well.' What is missing?", options:["The candidate's own task and specific actions, and a measurable result","Nothing: short, concise answers are what interviewers want","More detail about the technology, tools and systems that were involved in the outage","A description of how stressful and chaotic the outage felt"], answer:0, why:"STAR needs a brief Situation, the Task you owned, the Actions you personally took and a Result with numbers or a clear outcome. 'The team worked hard' hides what you did, which is what the question is trying to predict. Technical detail cannot replace your own actions."},
 {id:"p26m6q4", q:"A company asks you to 'fix our actual onboarding scripts' as a take-home exercise, estimated at 12 hours. How should you read this?", options:["Normal: take-home tasks are supposed to mirror the real day-to-day work exactly","A chance to impress, so spend extra time beyond their estimate","A red flag: it looks like unpaid production work; normal tasks take 2–4 hours","Normal, as long as they let you use your own tools and setup"], answer:2, why:"A normal take-home is a two-to-four-hour exercise such as a KB article or a CSV user-creation script. Fixing their real production scripts for twelve unpaid hours is free labor and a preview of how the employer treats people. Time-box any task to what was asked."},
 {id:"p26m6q5", q:"On the first recruiter call you are asked, 'What salary are you looking for?' What does the lesson recommend?", options:["Name the lowest figure you would accept, so you stay in the running","Deflect once by asking their budgeted range; if pressed, give a range you'd accept","Refuse to discuss pay at all until there is a written offer in hand, to keep leverage","Quote the BLS national median as a fixed number and hold to it"], answer:1, why:"Deflecting once often reveals the employer's budget and anchors the talk on their number. If pressed, a range whose floor you would truly accept protects you. Naming your minimum leaves money on the table, and flatly refusing can stall a recruiter who is on your side."}
],
"p26m7":[
 {id:"p26m7q1", q:"While using a city's permit portal normally, you notice that changing one digit in the URL showed another applicant's record. What is the right course?", options:["Check a few more IDs first, so the report can prove how much data is exposed","Post it publicly so the city is pressured to fix it quickly","Stop, open nothing more, and report it via the city's security contact","Say nothing, since reporting it could get you into legal trouble"], answer:2, why:"Deliberately opening more records you are not entitled to see is unauthorized access under the CFAA and state law, whatever the motive. Coordinated disclosure means stopping, reporting what you saw to the owner and not publishing before a fix. Silence leaves other applicants exposed."},
 {id:"p26m7q2", q:"A penetration test's rules of engagement cover 10.20.0.0/24 from October 1 to 14. On October 10 you notice a vulnerable-looking host at 10.21.0.5. What do you do?", options:["Test it lightly, since it is on the same network and the client would want to know","Stop, record what you saw, and get a written scope change before touching it","Test it, then mention it in the report as a bonus finding","Ignore it entirely and leave it out of the report"], answer:1, why:"Authorization is defined by the written scope, and 10.21.0.5 is outside 10.20.0.0/24, so testing it is unauthorized whatever the intent. Report the observation to the engagement contact and act only after a written amendment. Hiding it helps no one."},
 {id:"p26m7q3", q:"You hold A+, Network+ and Security+, all earned in the same three-year cycle. Under the CompTIA rule in the lesson, what keeps all three current?", options:["100 CEUs: 20 for A+, 30 for Network+ and 50 for Security+","20 CEUs for A+, since the higher certifications never expire","Retaking all three exams every three years","Renewing Security+ with 50 CEUs, which also renews the lower two"], answer:3, why:"CompTIA certifications run on three-year cycles, and renewing a higher certification renews the lower ones beneath it. Fifty Security+ CEUs therefore cover A+ and Network+ as well. Adding all three requirements together overcounts the work."},
 {id:"p26m7q4", q:"You have about 8 study hours a week after field shifts. Which plan does the lesson's evidence support?", options:["One 8-hour Sunday session rereading the week's notes and chapters","Nightly sessions after 1 a.m., highlighting the key text in each chapter","Three 60–90-minute phone-away blocks of spaced review and practice questions","Daily ten-minute video clips watched on breaks during field shifts"], answer:2, why:"Deep-work blocks, spaced repetition and retrieval practice beat rereading and highlighting, and sleep consolidates learning, so 1 a.m. cramming costs more than it gives. Two weekday evenings and a weekend morning turn eight hours into steady progress that survives bad weeks."},
 {id:"p26m7q5", q:"Two years into help desk with A+ and Network+, a colleague plans to go straight to OSCP+ and a penetration-testing job next year. What is the realistic advice?", options:["Build sysadmin or SOC depth first; pentesting usually takes five to seven years","Go for it: OSCP+ on its own qualifies you for most pentest roles","Skip OSCP+ and go directly to GREM for the reverse-engineering pay","Collect several more certifications as fast as possible first, then start applying"], answer:0, why:"OSCP+ assumes working networking, Windows and Linux administration and scripting, which the sysadmin and SOC stages build. The lesson's path puts pentesting at five to seven years. Racing through certifications without experience burns people out, and employers can tell."}
]
});
